@page "/components/datagrid/selection"

<DocsPage>
    <DocsPageHeader Title="DataGrid - Selection" Component="@nameof(VekDataGrid<T>)">
        <Description>
            Learn about single-row and multi-row selection, click-to-select behavior, selection checkboxes, and row disablement in <CodeInline>&lt;VekDataGrid&gt;</CodeInline>.
        </Description>
    </DocsPageHeader>
    <DocsPageContent>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Row Selection">
                <Description>
                    The <CodeInline>&lt;VekDataGrid></CodeInline> component supports both single and multi-row selection.<br><br>
                    Selection can be triggered either by row clicks or by checkboxes.<br>
                    Row clicks are enabled by default via the <CodeInline>SelectOnRowClick</CodeInline> property (default: <CodeInline>true</CodeInline>). 
                    This allows users to select a row by clicking anywhere on it.
                    Set it to <CodeInline>false</CodeInline> to leave selection to the checkboxes; the <CodeInline>RowClick</CodeInline> event is raised either way.<br>
                    To provide visual feedback and explicit selection controls, add a <CodeInline>SelectColumn</CodeInline> in the <CodeInline>&lt;Columns></CodeInline> definition. 
                    This renders a checkbox for each row.<br><br>
                    When using <CodeInline>SelectColumn</CodeInline> checkboxes, built-in accessible labels are provided by default and can be overridden with <CodeInline>AriaLabelFunc</CodeInline>.
                    <br><br>
                    Use the <CodeInline>MultiSelection</CodeInline> property (default: <CodeInline>false</CodeInline>) to control whether users can select one or multiple rows.
                    Single selection is enabled by default.
                    <br><br>
                    To change the selection from code, assign a new set to the bound <CodeInline>SelectedItems</CodeInline> instead of editing the existing one.
                    The grid detects a change by reference, so calling <CodeInline>Clear</CodeInline>, <CodeInline>Add</CodeInline> or <CodeInline>Remove</CodeInline> on the bound set does not update the grid's selection.
                    Likewise, assign a new list to <CodeInline>Items</CodeInline> when removing rows, so selected rows that were removed are dropped from the selection.
                    <br><br>
                    To disable selection for specific rows, use the <CodeInline>DisabledFunc</CodeInline> parameter on the <CodeInline>SelectColumn</CodeInline>.<br>
                    For example, rows 5 and 10 can be disabled by returning true for those rows in the function.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" Code="@nameof(DataGridSelectionExample)" ShowCode="false" Block="true" FullWidth="true">
                <DataGridSelectionExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

        <DocsPageSection>
            <SectionHeader Title="Focused Row and Keyboard Navigation">
                <Description>
                    Enable <CodeInline>FocusedRowEnabled="true"</CodeInline> to track a dedicated active row focus independently of selection.<br><br>
                    The focused row supports two-way binding via <CodeInline>@@bind-FocusedRowIndex</CodeInline> and <CodeInline>@@bind-FocusedItem</CodeInline>, as well as change notifications through <CodeInline>FocusedRowChanged</CodeInline>.<br><br>
                    When focused row navigation is enabled, users can traverse rows using standard keyboard commands:
                    <ul>
                        <li><CodeInline>ArrowDown</CodeInline> / <CodeInline>ArrowUp</CodeInline>: Move focus to the adjacent next or previous row.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>PageDown</CodeInline> / <CodeInline>PageUp</CodeInline>: Jump down or up by the configured page size.</li>
                        <li><CodeInline>Home</CodeInline> / <CodeInline>End</CodeInline>: Jump directly to the first or last row.</li>
                    </ul>
                    The grid automatically ensures the active row remains visible by scrolling it into view unless <CodeInline>AutoScrollToFocusedRow="false"</CodeInline> is configured.
                </Description>
            </SectionHeader>
            <SectionContent DarkenBackground="true" Code="@nameof(DataGridFocusedRowExample)" ShowCode="true" Block="true" FullWidth="true">
                <DataGridFocusedRowExample />
            </SectionContent>
        </DocsPageSection>

    </DocsPageContent>
</DocsPage>